 9, 2026 Read More\n\nFrontier AI 2025: The Year Enterprise AI Became Proprietary Intelligence 3 min read December 30, 2025 Read More\n\n12 per page\n\n1-12 of 173--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCodex 5x质保 (https://www.catalyzex.com/s/Codex%205x%E8%B4%A8%E4%BF%9D)\nciteturn60search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Experiments compare AReaL-RSI with Codex-Goal on downstream harness optimization, GPU kernel optimization, and AI research tasks using two backbones.\n## AReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvement\n\nRequest Code\n\nImage: Bookmark button\n\nImage: Alert button\n\nOct 13, 2026\n\nAuthors:Wenhao Zhou, Jiajun Song, Haitao Wang, Zipeng Qiu, Yingqi Peng, Ruida Xu, Jiale Li, Wenshuang Li, Shusheng Xu, Wei Dong(+4 more)\n\nAbstract:Scaling recursive self-improvement requires additional computation to produce useful research progress in reaching persistent objectives. Intuitively, more researcher agents can explore more hypotheses, but early evaluation scores might misrepresent their promise, shared findings could lose their applicability conditions, and useful procedures could remain confined to completed investigations. In this paper, we present AReaL-RSI, a framework for organizing a persistent research team of agents without updating backbone LLM weights. We formulate the scaling problem in terms of evaluated outcomes and whole-system resource expenditure. Concretely, a leader agent selects task-appropriate research orientations and guides ongoing investigations; conditional memory sharing distinguishes unresolved problems from supported findings and enables recipient adaptation. During recursive evolution, after each campaign, evidence-based review revises researcher orientation, knowledge, and skills for later campaigns. Experiments compare AReaL-RSI with Codex-Goal on downstream harness optimization, GPU kernel optimization, and AI research tasks using two backbones. Under the reported resource allocations, AReaL-RSI improves Terminal Bench performance by 2.2 percentage points for each backbone and increases mean KernelBench speedup by 32.2-36.8%; further scalability comparisons favor the design of AReaL-RSI over native scaling: combining experience sharing with specialized researcher roles yields 1.9-27.5% lower kernel latency than independent exploration and 1.3-33.9% lower latency than sharing alone across all seven comparable task-backbone pairs.\n\n* 12 pages \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pinkbubblebubble/awesome-rsi: A curated, evidence-aware collection of recursive self-improvement research, agents, harnesses, benchmarks, and safety work. · GitHub (https://github.com/pinkbubblebubble/awesome-rsi)\nciteturn60search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * OpenRSI / OpenMLE / Frontis-MA1 (2026) - A full-stack AI4AI release from Horizon Research, Frontis.AI, and Tsinghua University, joining executable task environments, learned improvement operators, long-horizon program evolution, and held-out transfer evaluation. ... 2026  | AReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvement  |  | Revises a persistent research team's orientations, knowledge, and skills after each campaign while the leader, review, and promotion protocol stays fixed.\n\nawesome-rsi\n\nPublic\n\n  * NotificationsYou must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork7 (7)\n  * Star44 (44)You must be signed in to star a repository\n\n## About\n\nA curated, evidence-aware collection of recursive self-improvement research, agents, harnesses, benchmarks, and safety work.\n\n### Topics\n\nagent-harnessai-safetyautonomous-agentsautoresearchawesomeawesome-listrecursive-self-improvementself-improving-agents\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\nCC0-1.0 license\n\n# ♻️ Awesome Recursive Self-Improvement\n\nA curated, high-signal index of recursive self-improvement (RSI) in AI: foundations, self-modifying agents, evaluation, and safety.\n\nLast reviewed: 2026-10-18.\n\nImportant\n\nRSI is stronger than ordinary iteration. This list distinguishes systems that improve a persistent part of themselves from systems that merely revise one answer. A recursive system must also improve, or repeatedly reuse, the mechanism that produces later improvements. 2026  | ReTrace: Cross-Stage Failure Attribution for Self-Improving Robotic Manipulation  |  | Attributes downstream robot failures to earlier decisions and consolidates validated corrections into reusable manipulation skills across physical trials.\n2026  | Heterogeneous Agentic Coevolution (HACE)  |  | Jointly trains a specialist model and learning agent while evolving their interaction harness; the co-evolution recipe itself remains fixed.\n2026  | Drafting Agents, Not Just Tokens: Speculative Recursive Self-Improvement for Agentic Systems ·  |  | Drafts successor agents before full evaluation and trains the improver from completed search trees while keeping final verification external and fixed.\n2026  | Verifier-Harness Co-Evolution for Training-Free Self-Improvement on Open-Ended Tasks ·  |  | Co-evolves executable verifier programs and persistent harness state, admitting verifier successors only against anchored response contrasts.\n2026  | AReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvement  |  | Revises a persistent research team's orientations, knowledge, and skills after each campaign while the leader, review, and promotion protocol stays fixed.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrk Codex Leak (https://www.catalyzex.com/s/Ork%20Codex%20Leak)\nciteturn60search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Experiments compare AReaL-RSI with Codex-Goal on downstream harness optimization, GPU kernel optimization, and AI research tasks using two backbones.\n## AReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvemen